<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Denianke Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1490 CE – 1776 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Fulani kingdom of the Senegal valley founded by the warlord Koli Tenguella, known to the Portuguese as the Great Fulo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1490 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koli-tenguella">Koli Tenguella</a> led a mass migration of herders and warriors from the south-eastern highlands toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> river. After campaigns against the declining <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and its neighbours, he conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-toro">Futa Toro</a>, the old land of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-takrur">Kingdom of Takrur</a>, and founded a dynasty whose rulers bore the title Satigi.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Denianke kings were warrior rulers whose power rested on cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> clan support. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was considered lax by the Muslim scholars of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-toro">Futa Toro</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toroodbe">Torodbe</a>, who resented heavy taxes and the rulers' failure to protect villagers from enslavement. In the 18th century Moroccan and Moorish forces intervened repeatedly in succession disputes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1776 a clerical revolution led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayman-bal">Sulayman Bal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-kader-kan">Abdul Kader Kan</a> overthrew the Denianke and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-toro">Imamate of Futa Toro</a>, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa's</a> first Islamic reform states. Epic songs about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samba-gelaajo-jeegi">Samba Gelaajo Jeegi</a>, a Denianke prince, remain popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulaar">Pulaar</a> oral literature.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/denianke-kingdom.jpg" alt="Denianke Kingdom" width="320" height="197"><figcaption>Image: Gabagool · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WestAfrica1625.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1490 CE – 1776 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Koli Tenguella, Samba Gelaajo Jeegi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam and Fulani traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pulaar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Fulo, Empire of Great Fulo, Denanke</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1633&amp;focus=denianke-kingdom" aria-label="Denianke Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4385 1365 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Denianke Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4705 1621L4587 1861"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4587 1861h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4705 1621h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4705 1621h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1633&amp;focus=denianke-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
